## CSV Parsing Functions

### Pattern 1: Parsing with MapFunction

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.functions.MapFunction;

public class CsvParseFunction implements MapFunction<String, MyEvent> {

  @Override
  public MyEvent map(String line) throws Exception {
    // Handle empty or null lines
    if (line == null || line.trim().isEmpty()) {
      return null;
    }

    try {
      String[] fields = line.split(",", -1); // Keep empty fields

      // Validate minimum field count
      if (fields.length < EXPECTED_FIELD_COUNT) {
        System.err.println("Invalid record with " + fields.length + " fields: " + line);
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Insufficient fields in CSV line");
      }

      // Parse fields - handle optional/empty fields carefully
      long timestamp = Long.parseLong(fields[0]);
      String missingInfo = fields[1];
      long jobId = Long.parseLong(fields[2]);
      int taskIndex = Integer.parseInt(fields[3]);
      String machineId = fields[4].isEmpty() ? null : fields[4];
      int eventType = Integer.parseInt(fields[5]);

      return new MyEvent(timestamp, jobId, taskIndex, eventType);

    } catch (NumberFormatException | ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
      System.err.println("Error parsing line: " + line + " - " + e.getMessage());
      throw new RuntimeException("CSV parsing error", e);
    }
  }
}

// Usage:
DataStream<MyEvent> events = lines
  .flatMap(new ParseFunction())  // Use flatMap to handle nulls
  .filter(event -> event != null);
```

### Pattern 2: Using FlatMapFunction (Better for Errors)

